{"id": "x0736", "pool": "know-arc", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The correct answer is A, moons, planets, and comets. Moon s reflect light from their host stars or the Sun, planet s reflect sunlight as they orbit, and come ts have bright tails that shine due to reflected sunlight. Stars, on the other hand, produce their own light through nuclear fusion in their core s, so they do not shine solely by reflecting light.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 21, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0744", "pool": "know-arc", "n_q_chunks": 20, "onset_chunk": 19, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "A, to generate an electric current. This setup is basically a generator. Moving the magnet back and forth in the coil creates a changing magnetic field, which induces an electrical current in the wire. That current can flow through the light bulb and make it light up. The other options don' t fit because moving a magnet doesn't increase its strength or heat the wire without electricity, and protons aren't involved here. So the answer is definitely A.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 21, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0752", "pool": "know-arc", "n_q_chunks": 21, "onset_chunk": 21, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So the question is, what does an electric fan convert electricity into? Well, when you turn on a fan, it spins the blades and moves air around. That's mechanical energy because of movement. But fans also make noise; that's sound energy. And all machines create some heat as they run because of friction and resistance, so there' s heat energy too. However, chemical energy isn't involved here because we're not burning anything or changing fuel. So the correct answer is C: heat energy, sound energy, and mechanical energy only.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 30, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0760", "pool": "know-arc", "n_q_chunks": 22, "onset_chunk": 22, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The answer is C. The person reading the temperature measurement does not have to be kept the same during the test. What matters for consistency are the location, the thermometer, and the time of day so that data can be compared properly. Different people reading it won't affect the accuracy as long as they use the same scale and method.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 17, "carrier_ended": null}
